  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "cvxpy.atoms.matrix_frac.MatrixFrac._grad",
    "observed_behavior": "Atom.grad for matrix_frac(X, P) returns silently wrong gradients when X is a matrix with more than one column, due to incorrect vectorization order in the gradient w.r.t. X.",
    "expected_behavior": "Gradient w.r.t. X must use CVXPY's column-major (Fortran) vectorization convention; the correct implementation removes the transpose so it vectorizes with DX.ravel(order='F').",
    "manifestation": "wrong_value",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "X has more than one column (matrix X with shape (n, p) where p > 1)",
      "Calling Atom.grad for MatrixFrac via matrix_frac(X, P)"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [
      {
        "input": "matrix X with shape (n, p) where p > 1 (more than one column) used in matrix_frac(X, P) / Atom.grad",
        "buggy_output": "Silently wrong gradient values for the gradient w.r.t. X, with errors up to ~0.97 (catastrophic).",
        "expected_output": "Correct gradient values; finite-difference check after the fix has max error ~1.85e-08 (vs ~0.97 before the fix).",
        "value_kind": "float",
        "source": "pr_body:PR#3247, pr_body:PR#3247 (Impact/Verification); diff:cvxpy/atoms/matrix_frac.py (the specific line change to use DX.ravel(order='F'))"
      }
    ],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "Before the fix: max error up to ~0.97 in a finite-difference gradient check.",
      "After the fix: max error ~1.85e-08 in the finite-difference gradient check.",
      "The buggy line `DX.T.ravel(order='F')` is described as equivalent to `DX.ravel(order='C')` (row-major), producing wrong gradients for p>1.",
      "The fix is to remove `.T` so it becomes `DX.ravel(order='F')` to match column-major vectorization convention."
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "matrix_frac(X,P) gradient w.r.t. X must be vectorized in Fortran (column-major) order per CVXPY convention",
      "Line 77 used `DX.T.ravel(order='F')`, which is equivalent to `DX.ravel(order='C')` (row-major order)",
      "This produces silently wrong gradients whenever `X` has more than one column (shape (n,p) with p>1); for single-column X the transpose is a no-op and the bug is masked",
      "Impact: `Atom.grad` returns incorrect values for `matrix_frac` with matrix X, with errors up to ~0.97 (catastrophic)",
      "Finite-difference check confirms the fix produces correct gradients (max error ~1.85e-08 vs ~0.97 before the fix)",
      "Fix is to remove the `.T` so the line reads `DX.ravel(order='F')`, matching column-major vectorization convention"
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "Reproduction must use matrix X with more than one column (p>1); with single-column X the bug is masked because the transpose is effectively a no-op",
      "Ensure the reproduction uses `Atom.grad`/`MatrixFrac._grad` path; the passage states this does not affect `problem.backward()` or `problem.derivative()` because they use `diffcp` internally"
    ]
  },
